// Dialogue exchanges, sixth set (kind 'exch', all six tags): trades, rituals, institutions and odd hours, mostly genre-free.
WLIB.add('exch', [
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 1, 'R: You\'ve parked in my space again.\nH: There are no names on the spaces.\nR: Everyone knows that\'s my space. I\'ve parked there for nine years.\nH: Then you should\'ve put your name on it.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You sold Mum\'s sewing machine.\nH: Nobody used it.\nA: I used it.\nH: You haven\'t sewn anything in years.\nA: I was going to. That was the point of it being there.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 3, 'R: You\'ll sign the tenancy back to me.\nH: I\'ve lived here {YEARS}.\nR: And never once complained about the damp.\n> {R} runs a finger along the windowsill and holds it up. Black.\nR: Which is why nobody will believe you when you start.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 2, 'R: That is my pigeon.\nH: It flew into my loft.\nR: It knows where it lives. It was confused.\nH: It\'s been here a month. It\'s laid an egg.\nR: Then I want the egg too.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 1, 'H: You gave my shift to your nephew, {R}.\nR: He needed the hours.\nH: So do I. I\'ve got rent on Friday and nothing in the fridge.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You ate the last of the wedding cake.\nH: It was in the freezer for a year.\nA: That\'s the tradition. You eat it on the first anniversary.\nH: I ate it on the first anniversary of me finding it.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 3, 'A: You weren\'t at the hospital.\nH: I was working.\nA: You could\'ve swapped.\n> {H} takes off {his} hi-vis jacket and lays it over the back of the chair. The sleeves are still damp from the night.\nH: I did swap. I swapped so I could be there for the next one.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 1, 'R: The court\'s given me the house, {H}.\nH: You never lived in it.\nR: I paid for it. That\'s what living in it means, legally.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 3, 'R: The marrow is too big.\nH: That\'s the point of the competition.\nR: There\'s a size where it stops being a marrow and starts being a statement.\n> {R} produces a tape measure and wraps it around the vegetable, which is the size of a small child.\nR: This is a statement.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 2, 'R: You were seen in the staffroom after hours.\nH: I was marking.\nR: In the dark?\nH: The light\'s been broken since September. I put in a request.\nR: There\'s no request.\nH: Then somebody took it.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 3, '> Two cars nose to nose in a narrow country lane. Neither moves. {H} winds down the window. So does {R}.\nR: There\'s a passing place behind you.\nH: There\'s one behind you.\nR: Mine\'s further.\n> {H} turns off {his} engine and opens a newspaper.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 1, 'H: You didn\'t come to Dad\'s funeral, {A}.\nA: I couldn\'t face it.\nH: Neither could I. I went anyway. I needed you there.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 2, 'R: Your boat\'s in my berth.\nH: My father kept a boat in this berth.\nR: Your father hasn\'t paid for it in three years.\nH: My father\'s been dead for three years.\nR: The harbour doesn\'t do condolences. It does invoices.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 1, 'R: You\'ve been singing too loud in the choir, {H}.\nH: That\'s how singing works.\nR: The others are complaining. They say they can\'t hear themselves being bad.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 3, 'R: Show me your hands.\n> At the border barrier, {H} holds out {his} hands, palms up. {R} looks at them a long time.\nR: These aren\'t a farmer\'s hands.\nH: I\'m a farmer.\nR: Then you\'ve been a farmer for about a week.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 1, 'A: You told everyone I was the one who flooded the bathroom, {H}.\nH: You were.\nA: You didn\'t have to tell the plumber\'s whole family.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You never call.\nH: You never pick up.\nA: Because you call at seven, when I\'m doing bath time.\nH: I call at seven because that\'s when the bus goes past your street.\nA: You could get off the bus.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 3, 'R: The jury believes me.\nH: They haven\'t decided.\nR: (straightening {rhis} tie in the corridor mirror) Look at them on the way in. They\'ve decided. They\'re just enjoying the lunch.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 2, 'R: You\'ve been using my wheelbarrow.\nH: It was in the communal garden.\nR: It was parked in the communal garden. That\'s different.\nH: What\'s the difference?\nR: Intention.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 1, 'A: You signed the contract without reading it, {H}.\nH: There wasn\'t time.\nA: There\'s a clause on page eleven that says they own your weekends.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 2, 'R: Your hens are laying in my shed.\nH: They like your shed.\nR: Then they can pay rent.\nH: They do. In eggs. You\'ve been eating them since March.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 3, 'A: You kept the ring.\nH: You threw it in the river.\nA: And you went in after it.\n> {H} turns {his} hand so the ring catches the kitchen light. There\'s still a fleck of mud in the setting {he} has never cleaned out.\nH: I was cold for a week.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 1, 'H: You knew the brakes were bad, {R}, and you still let him take the van out.\nR: I had a delivery.\nH: You had a delivery. He had a family.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 3, 'R: You cannot bring a goat to the school play.\nH: He\'s in the nativity.\nR: There was no goat at the nativity.\nH: (patting the goat, which is wearing a tea towel) There is now. He\'s been rehearsing.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 2, 'R: You\'re three days late with the rent.\nH: The bank\'s holding my wages.\nR: The bank\'s not my problem.\nH: It\'s not mine either. It\'s just where the money lives.\nR: Then go and get it out.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 3, '> A cramped office kitchen. {H} and {A} stand at the microwave, each holding a frozen dinner. The timer reads 4:00.\nA: I was here first.\nH: I was here first in my heart.\nA: That\'s not how the queue works.\n> {H} opens the microwave door and slides {his} dinner on top of {A}\'s.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 1, 'A: You missed the school play, {H}.\nH: I was stuck at the depot.\nA: She was a sheep. She waved at the empty chair.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 2, 'R: You\'ve been telling the families the factory\'s safe.\nH: You told me it was safe.\nR: I told you to tell them.\nH: That\'s the same thing.\nR: Not in court it isn\'t.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 1, 'R: Your cat\'s been sleeping on my car, {H}.\nH: He likes the warm engine.\nR: He\'s left paw prints on my bonnet in the shape of an insult.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 3, '> A ward corridor at three in the morning. {R} blocks the door of the drug cabinet.\nR: The numbers don\'t match.\nH: Then count again.\nR: I\'ve counted four times.\n> {R} holds out {rhis} hand for {H}\'s keys.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You\'ve rearranged my spices.\nH: Alphabetically.\nA: I had a system.\nH: Your system had cumin in three places.\nA: That\'s because I use a lot of cumin.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 2, 'R: The council\'s closing the library.\nH: People still use it.\nR: Twelve people.\nH: Twelve people every day. Some of them haven\'t got anywhere else to sit.\nR: We\'ll put benches in the park.\nH: In November?'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 3, 'A: You promised you\'d stop.\nH: I did stop.\n> {A} lifts the lid of the toolbox in the boot. Under the spanners: betting slips, dozens, all from the same shop, all dated this month.\nA: Then who\'s been stopping on your behalf?'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 2, 'R: You have entered your own scarecrow in the competition.\nH: Everyone made their own.\nR: Yours is wearing my coat.\nH: It was a very good coat for a scarecrow.\nR: It was a very good coat for me.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 1, 'H: You opened my exam results, {A}.\nA: The envelope came here.\nH: I wanted to be the first person to know I\'d failed. That was all I was going to get out of it.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'conflict', 1, 'R: You\'ve been bringing your own biscuits to the committee, {H}.\nH: Yours are stale.\nR: They\'re traditional. They\'ve been stale since nineteen-ninety.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 3, 'A: You gave away Grandad\'s medals.\nH: To the museum.\nA: They were ours.\n> {H} slides a small museum card across the table. Under the glass case\'s label, in small print: DONATED BY HIS GRANDCHILDREN.\nH: Now they\'re ours where everyone can see it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'conflict', 1, 'R: You\'ll lose your licence if you testify, {H}.\nH: I\'ll lose my mind if I don\'t.\nR: Minds don\'t pay mortgages.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'conflict', 3, '> The church fête. A tombola stall. {R} holds up a bottle of ketchup with a raffle ticket taped to it.\nR: This is the same ketchup you won last year.\nH: I donated it back.\nR: You\'ve donated it back four years running.\nH: Somebody has to want it.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You didn\'t tell me the van failed its test.\nH: It nearly passed.\nA: Nearly passed means failed.\nH: It means it\'s very close to legal.\nA: We\'re driving to Scotland in it tomorrow.'],
  ['Comedy', 'light', 'conflict', 2, 'R: Your hedge is three inches into my property.\nH: It\'s a hedge. It grows.\nR: Then tell it to grow in your direction.\nH: I\'ve tried. It doesn\'t listen to me either.'],
  ['Comedy', 'absurd', 'conflict', 2, 'A: You gave a eulogy at the wrong funeral.\nH: The names were similar.\nA: You talked about his love of sailing for ten minutes. He was a coal miner.\nH: Did anyone notice?\nA: His widow cried. She said she never knew.'],
  ['Drama', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 3, 'R: You took my son\'s place on the team.\nH: I earned it.\nR: You earned it by training on my pitch, under my floodlights, after hours.\n> {R} drops a bunch of keys on the bench beside {H}. {H}\'s own spare key to the clubhouse, the one {he} thought {he} had lost.'],
  ['Crime', 'tense', 'conflict', 2, 'R: The money\'s short.\nH: I counted it twice.\nR: Then you counted it wrong twice.\nH: Or someone took it between my hand and yours.\nR: Be careful which way you point that.'],
  ['Thriller', 'dark', 'conflict', 3, 'R: You weren\'t on the train.\nH: I was on the train.\nR: The guard says your seat was empty from Leeds onwards.\n> {H} takes a ticket out of {his} pocket. It has been clipped twice, once at each end. {He} only remembers once.'],
  ['Horror', 'tense', 'conflict', 1, 'H: You let them into the house, {A}.\nA: They knocked.\nH: I told you never to open the door after dark. Not even if they sound like me.'],
  ['Western', 'light', 'conflict', 2, 'R: Your mule drank from my trough.\nH: Mules don\'t read signs.\nR: He looked right at it.\nH: He can\'t read.\nR: Then he should learn. Or you should.'],
  ['Romance', 'tense', 'conflict', 3, 'A: You\'re seeing someone.\nH: No.\n> {A} holds up a coffee cup from the café on the corner, two sugars, lipstick on the rim. Not {A}\'s shade.\nA: Then you\'ve started wearing very brave colours.'],
  ['Sci-fi', 'dark', 'conflict', 2, 'R: Your oxygen allowance is cut by half.\nH: For how long?\nR: Until you stop asking questions about Deck Four.\nH: That\'s not a fair trade.\nR: Breathing never was.'],
  ['War', 'bittersweet', 'conflict', 1, 'A: You volunteered without telling me, {H}.\nH: I didn\'t want you to talk me out of it.\nA: I wouldn\'t have. I\'d have come with you.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'A: The bakery wants you on the night shift, {H}.\nH: I don\'t know how to make bread.\nA: Nobody does at three in the morning. You just stand near the ovens and nod.\nH: I can nod.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The removal van\'s booked for Thursday.\nH: Tell them to bring the small van.\nA: You\'ve got a four-bedroom house.\nH: I\'ve got a four-bedroom house of things nobody wants. I\'m taking the kettle and the photographs.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'R: Your number is forty-one.\n> {H} looks at the paper ticket, then at the screen above the counter. It reads 9. It has read 9 since {he} arrived.\nH: What happened to the other thirty-one?\nR: (stamping something) They went home.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The choir needs a bass by Sunday.\nH: I\'m not a bass.\nA: You\'ve got a low voice when you\'re tired.\nH: So you want me exhausted for the whole service?\nA: Just for the hymns.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m scared of the driving test tomorrow, {A}.\nA: You\'ve had forty lessons.\nH: And I\'ve failed four tests. The examiner knows my name. He sighs when he says it.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'re the new lifeguard?\nH: Starting today.\nA: Can you swim?\nH: I can swim very confidently in one direction.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: Your grandfather\'s tools are still in the shed.\n> {H} opens the shed door. Every chisel hangs in its painted outline on the wall. One outline is empty.\nH: He lent somebody the big one in nineteen-seventy-something.\nA: You want to find out who?\nH: I want to find out if they finished the job.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: I took the job at the funeral parlour, {A}.\nA: Are you all right with it?\nH: The dead are quiet. It\'s the families that frighten me.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll be judging the cake competition.\nH: I\'m diabetic.\nR: The judge before you was allergic to eggs. The one before that hated sugar on principle.\n> {R} hands {H} a small silver fork and a bucket.\nR: The bucket is for spitting. We\'ve had complaints.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The jury room\'s on the third floor.\nH: How long will it take?\nA: Depends on the other eleven.\nH: I\'ve met them. One of them brought knitting.\nA: Then it\'ll be long.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> {H} stands behind the market stall, a wall of cabbages at {his} back. {A} arrives with two coffees.\nA: How\'s the first morning?\nH: I\'ve sold one cabbage.\nA: That\'s not bad.\nH: To my mother.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'A: It\'s the last day of term, {H}.\nH: My last day ever. Thirty-one years of teaching.\nA: Will you miss it?\nH: I\'ll miss the noise. I don\'t know what quiet does all day.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'ll work the night desk alone.\nH: Alone?\nR: The porter left in March. He said the lift kept calling him to the fourth floor.\nH: What\'s on the fourth floor?\nR: Nothing. We closed it.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: Your neighbour has asked you to look after his bees, {H}.\nH: How many bees?\nA: He says about sixty thousand. He\'s given them all one name.\nH: What\'s the name?\nA: Gerald.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> The border hut. A single bulb. {R} turns {H}\'s passport over in {rhis} hands like a card {rhe} suspects is marked.\nR: Purpose of visit.\nH: Family.\nR: Whose?\n> {H} opens {his} mouth. Closes it.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve joined a darts team, {A}.\nA: You\'ve never played darts.\nH: They didn\'t ask. They just needed a fourth person who owned a coat.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The ferry\'s running its last crossing tonight.\nH: After how long?\nA: Ninety years. They\'re building a bridge.\nH: Then I\'m going across. Somebody should be on it who\'ll remember.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The farm\'s been sold.\nH: To who?\nA: A company. They\'ve sent a man with a clipboard.\n> Through the kitchen window, a stranger is counting the cows. He points at each one with a pen, as though signing for them.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'re the new mascot.\nH: I applied for marketing.\nR: Marketing is the mascot. The suit\'s in the boot room. It\'s a badger.\nH: Why a badger?\nR: We were very drunk when we chose.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: Your scan results come back today, {H}.\nH: I know. I\'ve been awake since four.\nA: Do you want me in the room?\nH: I want you in the room and I want you not to look at my face.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The hotel\'s fully booked. They\'ve given you the honeymoon suite.\nH: I\'m here for a plumbing conference.\nA: There are rose petals on the bed.\nH: Then the plumbers will have something to talk about.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, '> {H} lifts the last box onto the empty floor of the new flat. On its side, in {his} mother\'s handwriting: KITCHEN, MISC, DO NOT DROP.\nA: Want me to open it?\nH: Not yet. I want one box I haven\'t unpacked.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: The night shift on the ward is just me and one doctor, {A}.\nA: For the whole floor?\nH: For the whole floor. If two people die at once, one of them has to wait.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'A: The village needs someone to ring the church bell at midnight.\nH: Why me?\nA: You\'re the only one who can count to twelve without falling asleep.\n> {A} hands {H} a rope. It goes up into the dark. Somewhere very high, something rustles that is not a bell.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: Sign here.\nH: What is it?\nR: It says you were never in this room.\nH: I\'m in this room right now.\nR: Then sign quickly.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, 'A: The garage says your car is "interesting."\nH: Interesting how?\n> The mechanic waves them over and lifts the bonnet. Inside, carefully, someone has built a bird\'s nest. There are three eggs.\nH: Can it still drive?\nA: Only very gently.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: I can\'t believe Mum\'s selling the market stall, {A}.\nA: She\'s been there since before you were born.\nH: I learned to count on that stall. In apples.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The court date\'s set.\nH: When?\nA: The fourteenth. The same day as your daughter\'s school play.\nH: What\'s she playing?\nA: A tree.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: My uncle wants you to teach him to text, {H}.\nH: How old is he?\nA: Ninety. He wants to complain to the council faster.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> The waiting room clock ticks loudly. {H} sits opposite {R}. Both hold the same coloured form.\nR: What are you in for?\nH: Same as you, I\'d guess.\nR: (folding {rhis} form so {H} can\'t read it) I doubt that very much.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m going to cater my cousin\'s wedding, {A}.\nA: How many guests?\nH: Two hundred. I\'ve done the maths. I need about nine hundred sausage rolls and a miracle.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: You kept the shop sign.\nH: It\'s been over that door {YEARS}.\nA: Where are you going to put it?\nH: Above my bed. I want to wake up open for business.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'R: The boat leaves at four. Don\'t bring anything you\'d miss.\nH: What if I need it?\nR: Then you\'ll miss it twice.\n> {R} turns back to the harbour. The water is the colour of a coin left in a pocket too long.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'ve been chosen for the parish quiz team.\nH: I\'m terrible at quizzes.\nA: You\'re good at sport and biscuits. We\'re weak on both.\nH: There\'s a biscuit round?\nA: Every year. It\'s brutal.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: The inspector arrives at nine tomorrow, {H}.\nH: Then we\'ve got one night to fix a whole kitchen.\nA: And find the mouse.\nH: And find the mouse.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: Why are you wearing a hairnet?\nH: I start at the cheese factory on Monday.\nA: It\'s Saturday.\nH: I\'m getting used to it. It\'s a commitment.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: The care home asked if you\'d play piano for the Sunday tea.\nH: I haven\'t played since school.\n> {H} lifts the lid on the old upright in the day room. Inside, a note is taped to the strings: MIDDLE C STICKS. HIT IT HARDER. — DAD.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: My brother\'s lorry hasn\'t come back from the crossing, {A}.\nA: How long?\nH: Two days. He always calls from the services. Always.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'R: Welcome to the lost property office. You\'ll be cataloguing.\nH: What do people lose?\nR: Umbrellas. Gloves. One left shoe a week, never a right.\n> {R} opens a cupboard. Hundreds of left shoes, sorted by size.\nR: Nobody knows why. Don\'t ask the right shoes.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The coach wants you to take the penalty.\nH: I\'ve never taken a penalty.\nA: That\'s why. Nobody\'s seen how you do it.\nH: I don\'t know how I do it.\nA: Even better.'],
  ['Comedy', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: Your speech for the retirement do is four minutes.\nH: I can do four minutes.\nA: You\'ve written nine pages.\nH: I\'ll speak fast. He won\'t notice. He\'s been deaf in one ear since nineteen-ninety.'],
  ['Comedy', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> {H} stands in the shop doorway in a full morning suit. Behind {him}, a sign: DRY CLEANING, SAME DAY.\nA: Why are you dressed for a wedding?\nH: They lost my trousers. This was all they had in my size.\nA: Whose wedding suit is it?\nH: I\'m finding out at five o\'clock.'],
  ['Drama', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The headstone man wants to know what to carve.\nH: Just her name. And the dates.\nA: He says there\'s room for a line.\nH: She\'d want something about the garden. She\'d want it spelled right.'],
  ['Crime', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: You do the books for the laundrette.\nH: Every Friday.\nR: Then explain why it washes four hundred shirts a week in a street with forty people.\nH: They\'re very clean people.'],
  ['Horror', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The new house came with a dog bowl.\nH: The last owners must\'ve had a dog.\nA: They didn\'t. I asked.\n> {A} sets the bowl on the kitchen floor. In the morning, it is full of water. Neither of them filled it.'],
  ['Western', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'H: The stagecoach comes through at noon, {A}, and I don\'t trust the man who\'s riding guard.\nA: Why not?\nH: He keeps checking his watch. Nobody here owns a watch.'],
  ['Romance', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'re running the flower stall next to hers?\nH: It was the only free pitch.\nA: She sells roses. You sell cactuses.\nH: Somebody has to give the cautious people a choice.'],
  ['Sci-fi', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The ship\'s cook has quit.\nH: We\'re nine months from anywhere.\nA: He left a note. It says "the soup knows."\nH: Knows what?\nA: He didn\'t elaborate.'],
  ['War', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: They want a volunteer to carry the post up to the line.\nH: What\'s in it?\nA: Letters from home. And three telegrams.\nH: Do I have to hand them out?\nA: You have to watch them being opened.'],
  ['Period', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: Her Ladyship requires a new lady\'s maid.\nH: I\'ve only ever worked in the kitchen.\n> {A} takes {H}\'s hands, turns them over: burns, calluses, a scar across one thumb.\nA: Wear gloves. And never let her see you look at the cakes.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: So the thing about the allotment committee, right, is that they meet on Tuesdays, except when it\'s a Tuesday with a bin collection, in which case it\'s Wednesday, unless Brian\'s back is bad —\nH: {A}.\nA: — and you\'re the new secretary.\nH: I didn\'t apply.\nA: Nobody applies. You just miss a meeting.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: Dad\'s asked me to clear out his workshop, {A}.\nA: Is he moving?\nH: Into the home. He can\'t hold a hammer any more. He says he doesn\'t want to see it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: The night ferry has one rule.\nH: Which is?\nR: Nobody goes on the car deck after midnight.\nH: Why not?\nR: Because they come back and won\'t tell us.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> A school hall. Rows of empty chairs. {H} sits alone at the front, holding a recorder. {A} leans in the doorway.\nA: Where\'s the orchestra?\nH: I am the orchestra. The others got chickenpox.\nA: The concert\'s in an hour.\nH: I know eleven songs. Nine of them are the same song.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll translate for the defendant.\nH: I\'ll translate what he says.\nR: Exactly what he says.\n> {R} leans closer.\nR: Not what you think he means.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve been made captain of the pub quiz team, {A}.\nA: How?\nH: The old captain moved to Spain. He handed me the pencil at the airport. It was very moving.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The cinema\'s having one last screening.\nH: Of what?\nA: Whatever\'s in the projector. The owner says he hasn\'t checked in years.\nH: Get two tickets. Front row. We\'ll find out together.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: There\'s a man in A&E who keeps asking for you by name.\nH: I don\'t know anyone here.\nA: He knows your shift pattern. He knows you take your tea with two sugars.\n> {H} looks down at {his} mug. Two sugars. {He} hasn\'t told anyone that in {YEARS}.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: The council wants you to be the town\'s official Father Christmas, {H}.\nH: It\'s June.\nA: They plan ahead. They\'ve had problems with beards.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: Customs found something in your case.\nH: What?\nA: A jar of honey.\nH: That\'s a present for my aunt.\nA: They\'ve called a supervisor. And a dog.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> The car wash. {H} sits in the driver\'s seat, windows up, as giant brushes pound the roof. In the passenger seat, {A} is perfectly calm, eating crisps.\nA: You said this was the only quiet place to talk.\nH: Nobody can hear us in here.\nA: We can\'t hear us in here.\nH: (shouting) THAT\'S THE POINT.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: The orchard\'s ready.\nH: All of it?\nA: All of it. And we\'ve no pickers this year.\n> {H} looks down the rows. Every tree bowed, every branch heavy. A ladder lies in the grass where it was left last autumn.\nH: Then it\'s just us. Start at the end Mum liked.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: Somebody\'s been sleeping in the bus depot, {A}.\nA: How do you know?\nH: The back seat of the number nine is warm every morning. And it\'s never the same shape.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'R: You are applying to be a lighthouse keeper.\nH: Yes.\nR: The lighthouse is automatic.\nH: I\'d still like to keep it.\nR: Keep it from what?\nH: Loneliness, mostly.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: You\'ve got the big match tomorrow, {H}.\nH: I know. I\'m scared I\'ll let everyone down.\nA: You won\'t.\nH: I\'m the goalkeeper. There\'s nowhere else to put it.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: Mind the dough. It\'s alive.\nH: It\'s flour and water.\nA: It\'s {YEARS} old. It came over on a boat with my grandmother.\nH: Does it have a name?\nA: Not one I\'m allowed to say in the shop.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve got to sing at Gran\'s funeral, {A}, and my voice always cracks on the high bit.\nA: She won\'t mind.\nH: She\'d laugh. That\'s what I\'m scared of. I\'ll hear her laughing.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The quarry\'s flooded again.\nH: Since when?\nA: Last night. Nobody heard the pumps stop.\nH: The pumps don\'t stop by themselves.\nA: That\'s what the night man said. Then he went home and didn\'t come back.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'R: Welcome to the Guild of Clockmakers.\n> Forty clocks tick in the room. Not one of them agrees with another. {R} checks {rhis} wristwatch, frowns, and shakes it.\nR: We meet at seven.\nH: Which seven?\nR: That\'s on the agenda.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> A queue outside the embassy, four hundred people deep, in the rain. {H} is somewhere near the middle. {A} arrives with an umbrella and two folding chairs.\nA: Any movement?\nH: The man at the front left an hour ago. He was smiling.\nA: Is that good?\nH: Nobody\'s decided.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'A: The hairdresser says you\'re booked in for a "brave choice," {H}.\nH: I asked for a trim.\nA: He wrote "brave choice."\nH: Then I\'m not going in. I\'m not brave enough for his choices.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The school wants you to judge the sports day.\nH: I came last in every race there.\nA: Every single one?\nH: I came last with style. They gave me a sticker for "trying." I\'ve still got it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll check the meters on the estate.\nH: All of them?\nR: All except number thirty-two.\n> {R} hands {H} a ring of keys. Thirty-one keys. The hook for the thirty-second is bent straight, as if someone pulled it off hard.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The wedding band\'s cancelled.\nH: The reception\'s in four hours.\nA: We\'ve got a man with an accordion.\nH: Can he play love songs?\nA: He can play one love song. Very, very slowly.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'ve been called as a witness.\nH: I didn\'t see anything.\nR: You were standing next to the car.\nH: I was looking at my phone.\nR: Then you\'ll tell the court what was on your phone.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> The village hall. {H} stands on a ladder pinning up bunting while {A} holds the bottom.\nA: How many flags is that?\nH: Three hundred.\nA: And the fête\'s tomorrow?\nH: The fête was last Saturday. Nobody told me to stop.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: You\'ve still got your father\'s shop key.\nH: The shop\'s a phone repair place now.\n> {H} turns the brass key in {his} palm. The shop window opposite reflects them both, and behind the glass a boy is fixing a screen with tweezers, hunched exactly the way {his} father used to hunch.\nH: It still smells of solder in there. I checked.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: The hospital rang about Mum, {A}.\nA: Is she worse?\nH: They didn\'t say. They just said come now. They never say come now.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: Your landlord wants to know if you\'ll look after his parrot, {H}.\nH: For how long?\nA: Parrots live eighty years.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'A: The teacher wants to see you about Sam.\nH: What\'s he done?\nA: Nothing. He\'s done nothing for three weeks.\n> {A} slides an exercise book across the table. Every page is filled with the same small drawing: a door, closed.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'ve signed up for the five-a-side?\nH: I need the exercise.\nA: You\'ve brought a deckchair.\nH: I\'m a substitute. I\'m pacing myself.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m worried about leaving the old flat, {A}.\nA: The new one\'s bigger.\nH: But I know where all the creaks are in this one. I\'ll have to learn a whole new set of noises.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: The tribunal starts Monday.\nH: How long will it take?\nR: Until one of us runs out of money.\nH: I\'ve got some savings.\nR: I\'ve got a firm.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> {H} stands at the hotel reception, holding a key the size of a spoon. {R}, the night manager, does not look up.\nH: The room number on this key doesn\'t exist.\nR: It exists.\nH: The corridor goes from fourteen to sixteen.\nR: (still writing) Then knock very politely.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: The surgeon wants to talk to you before the operation, {H}.\nH: Is something wrong?\nA: She says it\'s routine.\nH: Nobody says routine unless they\'re scared of a different word.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m learning to drive a forklift, {A}.\nA: Why?\nH: There\'s a wedding in a warehouse next month. I\'m the ring bearer. It\'s a long story.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'ve kept every programme from every match.\nH: Since I was seven.\nA: That\'s a lot of losing.\nH: It\'s a lot of Saturdays with my dad. The losing\'s in the small print.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The shepherd says three sheep went missing on the top field.\nH: Foxes?\nA: No blood. No wool. The gate was shut.\n> {A} holds out the padlock. It is closed. It has been closed from the inside.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The bowling club needs a new member.\nH: I\'m thirty-one.\nA: You\'ll bring the average age down to eighty-six.\nH: Is that good?\nA: It\'s the youngest we\'ve ever been.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'re on the night shift at the signal box.\nH: On my own?\nR: There\'s a phone. It only rings if something\'s wrong.\nH: How often does it ring?\nR: Hope you never find out.'],
  ['Comedy', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: The office wants you to organise the Christmas party, {H}.\nH: Me? I hate parties.\nA: That\'s why. Last year\'s organiser loved parties. Nobody\'s found the photocopier.'],
  ['Comedy', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> {H} sits in the driving instructor\'s car, gripping the wheel, engine off. In the instructor\'s seat, {A} holds a clipboard.\nA: You can start the engine now.\nH: I\'m building up to it.\nA: You\'ve been building up for twenty minutes. A woman just walked her dog past twice.'],
  ['Drama', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: The factory\'s laying off the whole night shift, {A}, and I\'m the one who has to tell them.\nA: When?\nH: Tonight. At the break. While they\'re eating their sandwiches.'],
  ['Thriller', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'A: The hotel says you checked in last night.\nH: I\'ve never been to this hotel.\n> {A} lays the registration card on the table. {H}\'s name. {H}\'s handwriting. Room 211.\nA: You asked for a wake-up call at four.'],
  ['Fantasy', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The ferryman won\'t take coins any more.\nH: What does he want?\nA: A memory. A small one. He says the river\'s gone thin.\nH: How small?\nA: Your first pair of shoes. He\'s very specific.'],
  ['Action', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'H: The bridge is going to collapse, {A}, and there\'s a school bus halfway across.\nA: How long?\nH: Three minutes. Maybe two. Get the tow rope.'],
  ['Romance', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: You\'re doing the flowers for her wedding.\nH: She asked.\nA: And you said yes.\n> {H} lifts a single white rose from the bucket and cuts the stem a little shorter than the rest.\nH: I\'ll make it beautiful. I\'ll make it so beautiful she has to look at it.'],
  ['Sci-fi', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: Your shift on the station starts at oh-four-hundred.\nH: Doing what?\nR: Listening.\nH: To what?\nR: Something keeps knocking on the hull. From outside.'],
  ['Period', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The vicar\'s coming for tea.\nH: We haven\'t got any cake.\nA: We\'ve got a cake. It\'s just a cake from last month.\nH: Turn it upside down. He\'s short-sighted.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> A fishmonger\'s slab at dawn. {H} arranges mackerel in a fan. {A} watches, sipping tea from a flask lid.\nA: You\'re doing the face thing again.\nH: What face thing?\nA: Arranging them so they all look at the customer.\nH: People buy what looks back at them.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The tailor wants to know if you\'ll take on his shop.\nH: I can barely sew a button.\nA: He says that\'s fine. He says the shop knows the rest.\nH: That\'s not how shops work.\nA: It\'s how his works. Forty years of chalk on the floor.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: The ambulance can\'t get through the snow, {A}.\nA: How far out are they?\nH: Two hours. She hasn\'t got two hours. We\'re driving her ourselves.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'re here about the vacancy for Head of Queues.\nH: I thought it said Head of Accounts.\nR: It\'s a typing error. But you\'ve been standing here forty minutes. That\'s promising.\nH: Is there a desk?\nR: There\'s a rope. You hold one end.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The scaffolding\'s going up on the school tomorrow.\nH: During term?\nA: The roof can\'t wait.\nH: Who checked it?\nA: The man who checked it last time. Before the gym ceiling.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m the new piano tuner for the whole county, {A}.\nA: How many pianos is that?\nH: Two hundred and nine. Most of them are in pubs. Most of them are sticky.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, '> {H} unrolls a set of chef\'s knives on the steel worktop of an empty restaurant. {A} reads the closing notice taped to the window.\nA: Last service tonight.\nH: Then we cook everything.\nA: There\'s food for sixty. We\'ve got nine bookings.\nH: Then we feed the whole street.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: The patient in bed six asked for a priest.\nH: I\'ll call the chaplain.\nR: Not the chaplain. A particular priest. He gave a name and a parish.\nH: So?\nR: The parish burned down in nineteen-twelve.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'A: The new job comes with a uniform.\n> {H} unfolds it. It is a full-body chicken costume, the feathers slightly singed at the tail.\nH: What happened to the last one?\nA: Barbecue promotion. He stood too close to the product.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: The bailiffs are due at ten, {H}.\nH: Then we\'ve got two hours to get Mum\'s piano out the back door.\nA: It won\'t fit.\nH: It fitted in. It\'ll fit out.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The new boss says we have to do a team-building weekend.\nH: Where?\nA: A farm. We\'ll be herding geese together.\nH: I don\'t want to bond with anyone over geese.\nA: The geese don\'t want to bond with you either. It\'ll be very honest.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: It\'s my first Christmas without Dad, {A}.\nA: What do you want to do?\nH: I want to burn the sprouts the way he did. Exactly the same. On purpose.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The new tenants say they found a room behind the bookshelf.\nH: There\'s no room behind the bookshelf. I lived there for {YEARS}.\n> {A} holds up a phone. A photograph: a small bare room. A chair. On the chair, folded, a cardigan {H} lost in nineteen-ninety-something.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: Your nan\'s entered you in the beard-growing contest, {H}.\nH: I can\'t grow a beard.\nA: She knows. She\'s entered you in the "most hopeful" category.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'re the new night porter.\nH: Yes.\nR: You\'ll hear a lot. Guests fight. Guests cry. Guests leave.\n> {R} pushes a leather ledger across the desk.\nR: You write down who goes up. You never write down who comes down.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, 'A: The village hall\'s double-booked.\nH: With what?\nA: Your yoga class and the brass band.\n> Through the door: twenty people in a downward-facing pose, and behind them a man with a tuba waiting, very patiently, for his cue.\nH: We\'ll call it advanced.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The vet\'s asked you to bring him in tomorrow.\nH: For the jab?\nA: For the talk.\nH: He\'s still eating.\nA: She says that\'s the best day to have it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: Somebody\'s been moving the gravestones in the churchyard, {A}.\nA: Moving them how?\nH: Turning them. Every night, a few more. They\'re all facing the house now.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'R: Do you have experience in the egg industry?\nH: I\'ve eaten eggs.\nR: That\'s consumer side. This is grading.\nH: What do you grade them on?\nR: Attitude, mostly.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The plane can\'t land. Fog.\nH: How long can it circle?\nA: Forty minutes of fuel.\nH: And the next airport?\nA: Fifty minutes away.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve been asked to referee the under-nines, {A}.\nA: That\'s nice.\nH: It\'s not nice. Have you met their parents? One of them brought a megaphone.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: Your brother called. He wants the boat.\nH: The boat\'s rotten.\nA: He knows.\n> {H} looks out of the window at the hull on its trailer, the name half gone: only THE FAIR remains of THE FAIR WIND.\nH: Tell him he can have it if he comes and gets it himself.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: The audit starts at nine.\nH: Which accounts?\nR: All of them. Going back {YEARS}.\nH: That\'s before I started.\nR: That\'s why they want you. You\'re the only one who doesn\'t know what\'s there.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> A sorting office at four in the morning. {H} holds up a parcel wrapped in brown paper. It is ticking.\nA: Is that a clock?\nH: It says "fragile" and "do not wind."\nA: Who\'s it for?\nH: (reading) "Whoever\'s holding this."'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: The headmaster wants to see you in his office, {H}.\nH: I\'m thirty-eight.\nA: He was your headmaster too. He says you still owe him a detention.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The new kitchen porter doesn\'t speak English.\nH: How do I train him?\nA: Point and nod.\nH: I can do that.\nA: You\'ve already pointed at three wrong things.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: The chapel\'s closing after the last service, {A}.\nA: Will you go?\nH: I was christened there. I got married there. I should be there to turn the lights out.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The crane operator won\'t come down.\nH: Why not?\nA: He says he saw something from up there.\n> {H} shades {his} eyes and looks up. Two hundred feet above the site, the little cab is dark. In its window, a hand-written sign: DON\'T DIG BY THE WALL.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: The town\'s asked you to be the judge at the dog show, {H}.\nH: I\'m scared of dogs.\nA: They know. They think it\'ll make you fair.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'R: You\'re the new interpreter for the hearing.\nH: Which language?\nR: Both of them.\nH: Both of which?\nR: You\'ll find out when they start shouting.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> Monday morning. A primary school classroom. {H} stands before twenty-eight six-year-olds. {A}, the teaching assistant, holds out a stick of chalk.\nA: First day nerves?\nH: No.\nA: Then why are you holding the chalk like a knife?'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: Your mum\'s asked you to read at the wedding.\nH: Her wedding?\nA: Her second wedding.\nH: I read at her first one. I was six. I read the wrong page.\nA: She wants you to read the wrong page again.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m scared to go back to the mine, {A}.\nA: You\'ve done it for twenty years.\nH: I know. That\'s why I\'m scared. I know which bits of the roof have been talking.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The museum wants you to sit with the mummy overnight.\nH: Why?\nA: Insurance. Somebody has to be awake in the room.\nH: What happens if I fall asleep?\nA: Then there are two of you.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> The tyre shop at closing. {R} rolls a car into the bay and leaves the engine running.\nR: Four new tyres. Cash.\nH: We\'re closing.\nR: (laying notes on the counter, one at a time) Then you\'ll close a bit later.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'A: My grandmother\'s opening a café, {H}.\nH: Your grandmother can\'t cook.\nA: She\'s not doing food. She\'s doing gossip. Two pounds a cup.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, '> The ice rink, the week before it closes for good. The lights are half off. {H} stands at the barrier in hired skates, two sizes too big.\nA: You learned here?\nH: Over there. By the pillar. He skated backwards in front of me the whole first winter.\n> {H} steps onto the ice and does not let go of the barrier.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The vicar asked you to dig the new grave.\nH: Who\'s it for?\nA: He didn\'t say.\nH: What size?\nA: (handing over a slip of paper) He said yours would be about right.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'R: The council wants a new statue in the square. You\'re the sculptor.\nH: Of whom?\nR: We haven\'t agreed.\n> {R} unrolls a plan: a stone plinth with nothing on it, and beside it a note reading PROVISIONAL HORSE.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve got my first case in court on Monday, {A}.\nA: What\'s the charge?\nH: Stealing a goat. And I\'m scared the goat\'s going to be there.'],
  ['Comedy', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'re giving the eulogy for your uncle.\nH: He owed everyone money.\nA: Then keep it short.\nH: If I keep it short they\'ll think I\'m hiding the list.'],
  ['Comedy', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> The tiny back office of a magic shop. {H} holds a top hat at arm\'s length. Something inside is moving.\nA: You were meant to feed it on Friday.\nH: You never said what "it" was.\nA: That\'s because we don\'t know.'],
  ['Drama', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'H: I start teaching at the prison on Monday, {A}.\nA: Are you scared?\nH: Of the men? No. Of the one who already wrote to me asking for books.'],
  ['Crime', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The pawnshop owner wants you to value a ring.\nH: What\'s odd about it?\n> {A} sets the ring on the velvet pad. Inside the band, engraved, is a date. It is next Tuesday.'],
  ['Horror', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The night nurse says room nine\'s patient asks for you every night.\nH: Room nine\'s been empty for a month.\nA: That\'s what I said.\nH: And?\nA: She said it\'s been asking more politely lately.'],
  ['Western', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The circuit judge comes through Friday.\nH: That\'s three days.\nA: Long enough to build a gallows.\nH: Or a church.\nA: Same carpenter.'],
  ['Musical', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve got the lead in the town panto, {A}.\nA: What are you?\nH: The back half of the cow. But I\'ve got a solo.'],
  ['Animation', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The scarecrow\'s applied for a job in the city.\nH: As what?\nA: Doorman. He\'s very good at standing.\nH: He\'s made of straw.\nA: He says that\'s discrimination.'],
  ['War', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll take the radio up the hill tonight.\nH: Alone?\nR: The others are busy.\n> {R} looks out of the tent flap. There are no others. There haven\'t been for two days.'],
  ['Superhero', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The city\'s asked you to cut the ribbon at the new library.\nH: With my hands?\nA: With scissors. Like a normal person.\nH: I don\'t do normal ribbons. Last time I cut the building.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'ve been made godparent.\nH: What does a godparent do?\nA: Stand at the front. Hold the baby. Don\'t drop the baby.\nH: That\'s three things. I\'m good at two.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> The back room of a butcher\'s shop. {H} wears an apron that reaches {his} ankles. {A} hands {H} a cleaver and a single sausage.\nA: Start small.\nH: How small?\nA: That small. Then we\'ll talk about lamb.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The removal men want to know about the piano.\nH: Leave it.\nA: You can\'t leave a piano.\nH: The new people\'s little girl touched it. She played the one note I taught my sister. Leave it.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, '> The night bus, empty but for the two of them. {A} sits behind {H}, chin on the seat back.\nA: You\'ve gone past your stop.\nH: I know.\nA: That\'s three stops.\nH: I\'m not ready to be home yet. This bus does a loop.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'R: The family wants an open casket.\nH: That\'s not possible.\nR: I told them.\nH: Did you tell them why?\nR: I told them it\'s not possible. I let them imagine the rest.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The fishing boat came back with one more man than it left with.\nH: Who?\nA: Nobody knows him. He\'s dry. Not a drop of water on him.\n> Down on the harbour wall, a stranger in a neat grey coat sits on a lobster pot, watching the town as if he\'s been told to remember it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The farmer next door won\'t let anyone near his top barn.\nH: Since when?\nA: Since the storm.\nH: What\'s in it?\nA: He says hay. He\'s put three padlocks on hay.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: You\'re booked as the entertainment at a six-year-old\'s party.\nH: I\'m an accountant.\nA: The magician cancelled. They said anyone with a waistcoat.\nH: I\'ll do their tax returns.\nA: They\'re six.\nH: Then it\'ll be quick.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'R: You are the new conductor of the town brass band.\nH: I don\'t read music.\nR: Neither do they.\n> {R} opens the bandroom door. Twelve elderly men stand holding instruments, each pointing in a slightly different direction, waiting to be told which way is forward.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The hospital needs volunteers to be pretend patients for the trainee doctors.\nH: What do I have?\nA: (reading a card) Mild confusion and a sprained ankle.\nH: I already have mild confusion.\nA: Then you\'ve only got to learn the ankle.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: They\'ve closed the motorway.\nH: For how long?\nA: Nobody knows. The man in the next car is unpacking a stove.\nH: Ask him if he\'s making enough for two.'],
  ['Comedy', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: My dad\'s started a podcast about his allotment.\nH: Who listens?\nA: Me. Mum. A man in Norway who\'s very interested in his leeks.\nH: What\'s the next episode?\nA: "Slugs: The Reckoning." Six parts.'],
  ['Comedy', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> A tiny community hall. A sign reads LINE DANCING — BEGINNERS. {H} is the only person in the room under seventy. {A} hands {H} a cowboy hat.\nA: They\'ve been waiting all year for a new man.\nH: Why?\nA: The last one learned the steps too fast. They want someone they can teach.'],
  ['Comedy', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: Your aunt\'s planning her own funeral.\nH: She\'s fine.\nA: She wants it done before she\'s not. She\'s booked a band.\nH: What kind of band?\nA: Ska. And a bouncy castle shaped like a church.'],
  ['Comedy', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve been asked to give a toast at my parents\' fortieth anniversary, {A}.\nA: That\'s lovely.\nH: They argued the whole way through the last one. I\'m going to read out the scores.'],
  ['Drama', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The union wants you to speak at the gates tomorrow.\nH: Why me?\nA: Your father spoke at those gates in nineteen-eighty-four.\n> {A} lays a creased flyer on the table. Under the words, a grainy photograph: a young man with {H}\'s chin, mouth open, one hand raised.'],
  ['Drama', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: The hospice says she wants to go to the sea.\nH: She can\'t travel.\nA: They\'ll take the bed.\nH: The whole bed?\nA: On a lorry. With a nurse. She asked for chips.'],
  ['Drama', 'tense', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll present the case to the board on Friday.\nH: My case?\nR: Your case for keeping the ward open.\n> {R} closes {rhis} folder. Inside, {H} can see a printed slide titled CLOSURE TIMELINE with Friday already ticked.'],
  ['Crime', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The pickpocket\'s asked for you specifically.\nH: He\'s in custody.\nA: He wants to give you your wallet back. Said he took it at the station by accident.\nH: How do you pick a pocket by accident?\nA: He says it\'s a reflex.'],
  ['Crime', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'H: The garage owner says a car came in for a respray last night, {A}, and he\'s scared of the men who brought it.\nA: What colour was it?\nH: It was red. It went out grey. He says there was a dent in the shape of a person.'],
  ['Thriller', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> A conference hotel. Two hundred delegates, every one wearing an identical name badge reading HELLO. {H} looks at {his} own. It says the same.\nA: You\'re sure the contact is here?\nH: He\'ll be wearing a badge.\nA: Everybody\'s wearing a badge.\nH: Then he chose well.'],
  ['Thriller', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The safe-deposit box was opened last night.\nH: By who?\nA: The bank says by you. They have your signature.\nH: I was at my brother\'s.\nA: Your brother says you weren\'t.'],
  ['Horror', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The new caretaker says the school hamster is "not the original."\nH: What does that mean?\nA: He says there was a hamster in nineteen-seventy-one. He says it\'s still the same one.\nH: Hamsters live two years.\nA: That\'s his point.'],
  ['Horror', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: Your mother\'s house still has her answering machine.\nH: I keep it plugged in.\n> The red light is blinking. {H} has not recorded a new greeting. The cassette has not been changed. There is one new message.'],
  ['Action', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> The car park of a garden centre. {H} stretches beside a hatchback, cracking {his} knuckles. {A} leans on the trolley.\nA: You don\'t have to carry all of them.\nH: I said I\'d do it in one trip.\nA: That\'s forty bags of compost.\nH: It\'s a matter of honour.'],
  ['Western', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The town wants a new sheriff.\nH: What happened to the last one?\nA: He married the outlaw.\nH: Is that legal?\nA: It\'s legal now. He\'s the only one who could decide.'],
  ['Romance', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: Her husband\'s asked you to be the one to tell her.\nH: Why me?\nA: He says she\'ll believe you.\nH: She won\'t.\nA: He knows. He wants her to have someone to be angry at who isn\'t him.'],
  ['Sci-fi', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The colony has its first wedding.\nH: Who\'s officiating?\nA: You. You\'re the only one with a jacket.\nH: It\'s an engineering jacket.\nA: It\'s got buttons. That\'s ceremony enough out here.'],
  ['Fantasy', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'R: The castle needs a new taster.\nH: What happened to the old one?\nR: Nothing. That\'s why we need a new one.\n> {R} lifts a silver cover. On the plate beneath, a single grape, untouched, has been there for some time.'],
  ['Period', 'tense', 'setup', 2, 'A: The hiring fair\'s tomorrow.\nH: Who\'ll take me?\nA: Whoever needs a pair of hands.\nH: And if no one does?\nA: Then you stand there till the market closes and walk home in the dark.'],
  ['War', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: The ration book\'s down to the last page, {A}.\nA: Then we make the Christmas cake from carrots this year.\nH: Carrots and hope. My gran\'s recipe.'],
  ['Martial arts', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The old master wants you to sweep the hall.\nH: When does the training start?\nA: This is the training.\nH: For how long?\nA: Until the floor tells him you\'re ready.'],
  ['Documentary', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: The fishermen have agreed to talk to you on camera.\nH: All of them?\nA: The three that are left.\n> On the quay, three men in yellow sit on upturned crates, arms folded, watching {H} unpack the tripod with the same expression they give the weather.'],
  ['Musical', 'absurd', 'setup', 2, 'A: The choir needs a tenor. Tonight.\nH: I can\'t sing.\nA: The tenor part is just one note. It\'s long.\nH: How long?\nA: Three pages.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 1, 'A: You\'re helping your sister pick a wedding dress, {H}.\nH: Why me?\nA: She says you have no taste. She wants to watch you be wrong.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'m going to empty Dad\'s allotment shed tomorrow, {A}.\nA: Want help?\nH: I want you to stand there when I find his flask. He always left half a cup in it.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 1, 'A: The harbour master says no boats go out tomorrow, {H}.\nH: The weather\'s fine.\nA: He says it\'s fine every year on this date. And every year, nobody goes out.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'H: I\'ve been made chairman of the neighbourhood watch, {A}.\nA: What do you watch?\nH: Mostly Mrs. Purvis. She watches everyone else.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 1, 'A: The ferry won\'t leave if the wind gets any stronger, {H}.\nH: Then it has to leave now.\nA: Why now?\nH: The wedding\'s on the island at noon. And I\'ve got the rings.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 3, '> Five a.m. The flower market. {H} stands among buckets of tulips holding a list, while {A} haggles in a language neither of them speaks.\nH: What\'s he saying?\nA: I think he\'s saying no.\nH: Then why are you smiling?\nA: I think I\'m saying yes.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 3, 'A: Your old teacher\'s in the hospital. She asked for you.\nH: She failed me twice.\n> {A} holds out a hospital visitor\'s sticker with {H}\'s name already written on it, in red pen, in careful capitals.\nA: She marked your name.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'R: You\'ll be the jury foreman.\nH: I didn\'t ask to be.\nR: You were the only one who didn\'t look at the defendant.\n> {R} sets a small stack of folders in front of {H}. On top, a photograph of a family on a beach, smiling, one face scratched out with a coin.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, '> The laundrette. Midnight. Every machine is spinning. {H} sits on the bench with a single sock in a bag. {A} comes in holding another single sock.\nA: Is this yours?\nH: It\'s not a pair with mine.\nA: I know. I\'ve been doing this for a week. Nobody\'s socks match.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> A cramped tax office. {R} takes off {rhis} glasses and pinches {rhis} nose.\nR: Your returns are very neat.\nH: Thank you.\nR: Too neat. Nobody\'s expenses come to the same number every month.'],
  ['*', 'light', 'setup', 2, 'A: The shop needs someone to work Sunday mornings.\nH: Who comes in on a Sunday?\nA: Men buying newspapers and pretending they came for milk.\nH: How do I serve them?\nA: Pretend you believe them.'],
  ['*', 'bittersweet', 'setup', 2, 'A: Your mother wants you to learn the family recipe.\nH: She\'s never written it down.\nA: She says she\'ll cook it once, slowly, and you watch.\nH: Once?\nA: She says that\'s how she learned it. Then her mother died.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 2, 'A: The new tenants want to dig up the garden.\nH: They can\'t.\nA: It\'s their garden now.\nH: There\'s a dog under the apple tree.\nA: Only a dog?\nH: (beat) Tell them to dig by the fence.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 1, 'A: Your boss wants you to dress as a giant tooth for the dental fair, {H}.\nH: Which tooth?\nA: A molar. They\'re the friendliest.'],
  ['*', 'dark', 'setup', 3, 'A: The night shift at the bakery found a key in the dough.\nH: Whose key?\n> {A} lays it on the flour-dusted counter. It is very old, iron, warm from the oven. On its tag, in pencil: {PLACE}.'],
  ['*', 'tense', 'setup', 3, '> The bus shelter outside the job centre. {R} sits at the far end of the bench with the same brown envelope on {rhis} knee as {H}.\nR: Interview at ten?\nH: Ten.\nR: There\'s one job.\n> Neither of them opens the envelope. Neither of them moves along the bench.'],
  ['*', 'absurd', 'setup', 3, 'A: The hotel\'s lost a guest.\nH: Lost how?\nA: He checked in. He never checked out. His bill keeps growing.\n> {A} hands over a room-service docket from this morning: one boiled egg, one newspaper, signed with a neat little drawing of a door.'],
]);
